From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 14:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: Register the builtin classes without waiting on another thread.

register_builtin_classes() ran its body in a pthread_once. The body takes the loader lock: it loads each class's cursor
through a callback into user32, and user32's part (NtUserInitBuiltinClasses) loads uxtheme.dll. A thread that holds the
loader lock, in a DllMain, and creates its first window waited in the once for the thread that held the once and waited
for the loader lock: neither could go on. A Source engine game's vgui2.dll initializer does this (it calls OleInitialize
under the loader lock while another thread creates the window of a plug and play device).

No thread waits now. The first runs the registration and user32's part, and says it is done when it has; a thread that
arrives while it runs registers the classes itself, which refuses a class already there and drops its cursor, and leaves
user32's part to the first. Once done, a call is a load and a compare.

The problem was found by reading bornaradusin/PC5 (LGPL-2.1-or-later; patch 0066 of its patches/prospero-win, carrying
Leo Lobato's prospero-win patch 0981); this change is written independently here (NOTICE.md, "PC5").
---
 dlls/win32u/class.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/class.c b/dlls/win32u/class.c
index dc1a42b..e2d4dc9 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/class.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/class.c
@@ -1218,7 +1218,7 @@ static void register_builtin( const struct builtin_class_descr *descr )
         NtUserDestroyCursor( class.hCursor, 0 );
 }
 
-static void register_builtins(void)
+static void register_builtins( BOOL init_user32 )
 {
     ULONG ret_len, i;
     void *ret_ptr;
@@ -1236,7 +1236,7 @@ static void register_builtins(void)
 
     for (i = 0; i < ARRAYSIZE(builtin_classes); i++) register_builtin( &builtin_classes[i] );
     register_builtin( &edit_class );
-    KeUserModeCallback( NtUserInitBuiltinClasses, NULL, 0, &ret_ptr, &ret_len );
+    if (init_user32) KeUserModeCallback( NtUserInitBuiltinClasses, NULL, 0, &ret_ptr, &ret_len );
 }
 
 /***********************************************************************
@@ -1244,8 +1244,20 @@ static void register_builtins(void)
  */
 void register_builtin_classes(void)
 {
-    static pthread_once_t init_once = PTHREAD_ONCE_INIT;
-    pthread_once( &init_once, register_builtins );
+    /* 0: nobody started, 1: one thread is registering and running user32's part, 2: done. A thread that finds it
+     * running does not wait for it: the body loads the classes' cursors and user32's part loads uxtheme, through
+     * callbacks that take the loader lock, so a thread holding the lock (in a DllMain) that makes its first window
+     * would wait for a thread that waits for the lock. It registers the classes itself instead (one that is already
+     * there is refused, and its cursor dropped), and leaves user32's part to the first. */
+    static LONG state;
+
+    if (ReadAcquire( &state ) == 2) return;
+    if (!InterlockedCompareExchange( &state, 1, 0 ))
+    {
+        register_builtins( TRUE );
+        InterlockedExchange( &state, 2 );
+    }
+    else register_builtins( FALSE );
 }
 
 /***********************************************************************
